In article <11208@nlm-mcs.arpa> nicholas@mcs.nlm.nih.gov 
(Nicholas Tolstoshev) writes:
>What exactly do luckstones do?  Is there any difference between a 
>cursed/blessed luckstone and an uncursed one?

  In the old versions of nethack (2.3 and earlier), when you did
something to change your luck, it stayed changed forever.  With
3.0 this is no longer true:  luck "times out" now.  It has a
"resting value" of 0 (+1 if there is a full moon, -1 if there is
a new moon), and after you have done something to change it,
every 600 turns it moves by 1 toward its resting value.  (This
is reduced to 300 turns if the gods are angry at you.)

  The effect of a luckstone is to stop luck from timing out.  A
blessed luckstone preserves good luck; a cursed luckstone preserves
bad luck; and an ordinary uncursed luckstone preserves both.

  A luckstone does not in itself add to your luck, it only keeps it
from decaying.

  In my opinion a luckstone is probably the single most valuable
item in the whole game.  Once you have one, you can quickly max out
your luck by sacrificing or by throwing jewels to unicorns, and then
praying at altars becomes extremely rewarding.

	-- Bill Skaggs


